To begin using my newfound skills in data analytics, I decided to use data from an activity I was familiar with, bike sharing.
Being a D.C. native, I am quite familiar with Capital Bike Share, but I never thought of the outlying reasons as to why on some days there were more or less bikes available at docking stations near me.
This project analyzes how external factors such as weather, temperature, and day influence the use of bike sharing.
The dataset, extracted from Kaggle by Vivian YF Wang, contains the amount of rides that day, temperature, whether it is a workday or not, whether it is a holiday or not, air pressure, amount of rain or snow, humidity, wind, and the amount clouds per day. 

Due to this being my first project, I was stumped with a lot of the coding. I was more familiar with python, so I wanted to challenge myself and focus on using SQL for this project.
To simplify my confusion, I researched which codes to use for the specific results and learned what key words that SQL uses. 
I used SQL to clean the data by grouping the data, ensuring that there were no null values to skew the results, and providing results suited for human reading.
I then took my cleaned data to Tableau where I created 3 visualizations and a dashboard of all 3 visualizations. The first visualization illustrated the correlation of time of year, weather, and the amount of rentals in that month.
The second visualization illustrated the amount of rentals over the given span and whether they fell on a weekend, holiday, or workday.
The third visualization shows the amount of rides per category of temperature given cold, cool, warm, and hot weather. 
To build off on this, in the future I plan to research which aspects of Capital Bikeshare are most liked and disliked among consumers to maximize user satisfaction.

From analyzing this data, I took note of four key insights. First, snow decreases the amount of rentals, yet rain does not have much of a significant alteration.
Second, Capital Bike Share is most used when it is hot (above 80° F), followed by warm weather (60°-80°), then cool (40°-60°), and least used when it is cold (under 40°).
Initially, I hypothesized that the bike share would be most used on weekdays where it is warm but not hot.
I suspect that is due to the amount of tourists as well during these warm seasons, causing an increase in rentals.
I would recommend increasing the support services in the warmer and tourist seasons, as the bikes will be in high demand with higher rates of maintenance.
This would ensure a more maximized amount of rentals as the availability would not hinder riders.
One thing I learned during this as a general skill thought to have, to document my SQL queries in a notebook.
Initially, I did not save any of my queries and I had to redo my project in order to document my queries. It was inefficient but it allowed me to practice my queries further. 

